##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ben Mussett|title='As ugly as ugly can be': Why are there still temporary barriers around Union Station four years later?|work=w:Toronto Star|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230826205146/https://www.thestar.com/news/gta/as-ugly-as-ugly-can-be-why-are-there-still-temporary-barriers-around-union-station/article_2341a141-cc58-573b-ad7e-f9418675c504.html|location=Toronto, ON|publisher=w:Toronto Star Newspapers Ltd.|date=7 October 2022|issn=0319-0781|oclc=1081127642|passage=Within hours of the deadly van attack on April 23, 2018, the city installed a series of thigh-high '''concrete''' barriers around Union Station and other bustling spots in downtown Toronto.}}
# {{lb|en|logic}} A [[term]] designating both a [[quality]] and the [[subject]] in which it exists; a concrete term.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Sergeant (priest)|J[ohn] S&#91;ergeant&#93;]]|title=Solid Philosophy Asserted Against the Fancies of the Ideists:{{nb...|Or, the Method to Science Further Illustrated: With Reflexions on Mr. Locke's Essay Concerning Human Understanding}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Roger Clavil{{nb...|at the Peackock,}} Abel Roper{{nb...|at the Black Boy, both in Fleetſtreet, and}} Thomas Metcalf,{{nb...|over againſt Earl's-Court in Drury-Lane, 1697.}}|year=1697|page=91|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/solidya00serg/page/n148/mode/1up|passage=Whence follows, that the Abſtract Terms, [''Entity''] or [''Eſſence''] do properly ſignify [''A Capacity of Being''.] Tho' Entity is often us'd as a '''Concrete''' for the ''Thing it ſelf''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Leonard Peikoff|title=[[w:The Ominous Parallels|The Ominous Parallels: The End of Freedom in America]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Stein and Day|Stein and Day/Publishers]]|year=1982|page=331|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/ominousparallels00peik/page/331/mode/1up|isbn=0-812-82850-X|passage=Conceptualization is man's method of organizing sensory material. To form a concept, one isolates two or more similar '''concretes''' from the rest of one's perceptual field, and integrates them into a single mental unit, symbolized by a word.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Avi Sion|title=Future Logic|year=1990|page=344|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/FutureLogic/page/n345/mode/1up|passage=With regard to the physical domain, '''concretes''' are as a rule perceived through the senses.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Trinity in Process: A Relational Theology of God|editors=w:Joseph A. Bracken; w:Marjorie Hewitt Suchocki|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Continuum International Publishing Group|Continuum]]|year=1997|page=154|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/trinityinprocess0000unse/page/154/mode/1up|isbn=0-826-40878-8|passage=However, how can such a structure of '''concretes''' and abstracts be made evident, which after all means that knowledge always aims at the concrete, the unprecedented, the irreducibly dissimilar, although cognition always happens in developing similarity through abstraction?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Alan Musgrave|chapter=Pleonastic Platonism|title=From Truth to Reality: New Essays in Logic and Metaphysics|editor=Heather Dyke|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Routledge|year=2009|page=79–80|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fromtruthtoreali0000unse/page/79/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-415-98845-2|passage=On the right-hand sides we have sentences asserting that an equivalence relation holds between '''concretes'''—that is, that they are identical in some respect.}}
# {{lb|en|US}} A [[dessert#Noun|dessert]] of [[frozen custard#Noun|frozen custard]] with various [[topping#Noun|toppings]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Lutz (mystery writer)|John Lutz]]|title=Diamond Eyes|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Thomas Dunne Books|year=1990|page=170|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/diamondeyes00lutz/page/170/mode/1up|isbn=0-312-05074-7|passage=When Nudger and Claudia were finished eating they drove to the Ted Drewes frozen custard stand on Chippewa and stood in line for a couple of chocolate chip '''concretes'''. Drewes's '''concretes''' were delicious custard concoctions so thick that before the kids working behind the counter handed them to customers, they turned the cups upside down to demonstrate that the contents wouldn't pour out.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jonanna Widner|title=Dallas & Fort Worth|series=w:Moon Handbooks|location=Berkeley, C.A.|publisher=w:Avalon Travel|year=2013|page=86|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/moonhandbooksdal0000widn/page/86/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-612-38526-6|passage=Paradoxically richer and yet lighter than ice cream, frozen custard is softly served, and at Curly's you can have your I vanilla or chocolate flavor custard "'''concrete'''" style, with your choice of a rainbow of candy and fruit toppings whipped in.}}
#* {{RQ:WaPo|author=Candy Sagon|title=Nielsen's Frozen Custard in Vienna|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/lifestyle/food/2003/07/16/nielsens-frozen-custard-in-vienna/7bf574d9-e175-4af1-9e08-dc518ae0e759/|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251106103829/https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/lifestyle/food/2003/07/16/nielsens-frozen-custard-in-vienna/7bf574d9-e175-4af1-9e08-dc518ae0e759/|archivedate=6 November 2025|date=16 July 2003|passage=Once there, she opts for a '''concrete''' -- Nielsen's thick, spoonable, frozen vanilla custard mixed with add-ins like Oreos or strawberries or chocolate chips and piled into a 16-ounce cup for $4.35. She can't eat it all, of course, which is why a couple of friends need to come along as well.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Allie Chanthorn Reinmann|title=The Difference Between Milkshakes and Concretes (and How to Make Them)|work=w:Lifehacker|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230608020935/https://lifehacker.com/the-difference-between-milkshakes-and-concretes-and-ho-1850424489|date=11 May 2023|passage=A '''concrete''' has some distinct differences from a milkshake, specifically, the custard base mixture, the final texture, and the mix-ins. Technically, the common ice cream you buy at the store and use in a regular milkshake is made from a custard base. A custard is dairy thickened with the help of heated whole eggs or egg yolks, and a '''concrete''' uses a custard base that has a higher ratio of egg yolks in the recipe than the average ice cream.}}
# {{senseid|en|semi-solid extract}} {{lb|en|perfumery}} An [[extract#Noun|extract]] of herbal materials that has a semi-solid consistency, especially when such materials are partly [[aromatic#Adjective|aromatic]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Julia Lawless|title=The Encyclopedia of Essential Oils: A Complete Guide to the Use of Aromatics in Aromatheraapy, Herbalism, Health & Well-Being|location=Shaftesbury, Dorset;{{nb...|Rockport, M.A.; Brisbane, Qnd.}}|publisher=[[w:HarperCollins|Element]]|year=1992|page=37|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/encyclopediaofes0000lawl_y8d6/page/37/mode/1up|isbn=1-852-30311-5|passage=Most '''concretes''' contain about 50 per cent wax, 50 per cent volatile oil, such as jasmine; in rare cases, as with ylang ylang, the '''concrete''' is liquid and contains about 80 per cent essential oil, 20 per cent wax. The advantage of '''concretes''' is that they are more stable and concentrated than pure essential oils.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Celia Lyttelton|title=The Scent Trail: An Olfactory Odyssey|location=London{{nb...}}|publisher=w:Bantam Books|year=2007|page=37|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/scenttrailolfact0000lytt/page/37/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-593-05114-6|passage=Monsieur Roca held another '''concrete''' under my nose and asked if it reminded me of tea. I breathed in a refreshing green note of verbena, a smell that was so quintessentially English that I felt suddenly nostalgic. It was a daffodil scent; it symbolized spring and the hope that spring always brings. And finally he held out the mimosa '''concrete''' for me. As I breathed in its heady aroma I forgot all about the noxious fumes I'd inhaled as I'd walked towards the Robertet factory.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=David G. Williams|title=The Chemistry of Essential Oils: An Introduction for Aromatherapists, Beauticians, Retailers and Students|edition=second|location=Port Washington, N.Y.; Weymouth, Dorset|publisher=Micelle Press|year=2008|page=226|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/chemistryofessen0000will/page/226/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-870-22831-2|passage='''''Concretes''''', the waxy extracts produced by solvent extraction, were first introduced by the house of Roure, Bertrand Fils in Grasse, in 1873, and in 1888 Joseph Robert succeeded in developing a large-scale process for the solvent extraction of fragrant plants. This process was brought into commercial production two years later.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Karen Gilbert|title=Perfume: The Art and Craft of Fragrance|location=London; New York, N.Y.|publisher=CICO Books|year=2013|page=67|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/perfumeartcrafto0000gilb/page/67/mode/1up|isbn=1-782-49044-2|passage=Once the material is exhausted, the solvent containing the dissolved essential oil is distilled. This process removes the solvent, leaving behind the extracted matter, which is known as a '''concrete'''. The '''concrete''' is processed further to produce an absolute for use in perfumery.}}
# {{lb|en|possibly|obsolete}} [[sugar#Noun|Sugar]] [[boil#Verb|boiled]] down from [[cane#Noun|cane]] [[juice#Noun|juice]] to a solid mass.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Sugar Question:{{nb...|Being a Digest of the Evidence Taken Before the Committee on Sugar and Coffee Plantations which was Moved for by Lord G. Bentinck, M.P., 3rd February, 1848.}}|location=London|publisher=w:Smith, Elder and Co.|year=1848|volume_plain=Part II|page=115|pageurl=https://books.google.ca/books?id=AasI6ChI5M4C&pg=PA115|passage=The '''concrete''' is made by ingredients which are to remove the feculencies from the cane-juice as soon as expressed from the mill and which check fermentation; indeed juice may be kept for a week after the canes have been gruond, without turning acid, when these ingredients have been used.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Edward W[iley] Duckwall|title=Semi-Monthly Report of National Canners' Laboratory|journal=The Canner and Dried Fruit Packer|location=Chicago, I.L.|publisher=The Canner Publishing Company|date=18 August 1910|volume=XXXI|issue=6|page=24|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_prepared-foods_1910-08-18_31_6/page/24/mode/1up|issn=0096-0705|oclc=1421235|passage=Also molasses in the definition refers only to the product separated from the various sugar '''concretes''' specified in the purification of these raw sugars, while in trade terms what is defined under sugar cane syrup in the standards is often called molasses, the term "open kettle molasses" being used in this connection to indicate that the cane juice has been simply boiled down in open kettles.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=An Industrial Waste Guide to the Cane Sugar Industry|year=1959|page=1|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/canesugarindustr018786mbp/page/n6/mode/1up|passage=In some areas of the Far East, for example, factories producing sugar concrete may process as little as one ton of sugar cane per day and a total of not over 100 tons of sugar cane per year. From this we go to the other extreme where factories in the West Indies and Mexico process as much as 20,000 tons of sugar cane per day and 2 to 3 million tons of sugar cane per year.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Lendal H[enry] Kotschevar|title=Quantity Food Purchasing|edition=second|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:John Wiley & Sons|year=1975|page=352|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/quantityfoodpurc0000kots/page/352/mode/1up|isbn=0-471-50524-2|passage=Maple sugar is crystallized from the concentrated sap of maple. Maple '''concrete''' can be purchased and water added to make maple syrup.}}
# {{senseid|en|obsolete concretion}} {{lb|en|obsolete}} Any [[solid#Adjective|solid]] [[mass#Noun|mass]] formed by the [[coalescence#Noun|coalescence]] of separate [[particle#Noun|particles]]; a compound substance, a [[concretion#Noun|concretion]].
#* {{RQ:Boyle Sceptical Chymist|page=26|passage=And firſt, if I would now deal rigidly with my Adverſary, I might here make a great Queſtion of the very way of Probation which he and others employ, without the leaſt ſcruple, to evince, that the Bodies commonly cali'd mixt, are made up of Earth, Air, Water, and Fire, which they are pleas'd alſo to call Elements; namely that upon the ſuppos'd ''Analyſis'' made by the fire, of the former ſort of '''''Concretes''''', there are wont to emerge Bodies reſembling thoſe which they take for the Elements.}}
#* {{RQ:Hooke Micrographia|page=13|passage=Of this kind, I ſuppoſe, the ''Æther'', that is the ''medium'' or ''fluid'' body, in which all other bodies do as it were ſwim and move; and particularly, the ''Air'', which ſeems nothing elſe but a kind of ''tincture'' or ''ſolution'' of terreſtrial and aqueous particles ''diſſolv'd'' into it, and agitated by it, juſt as the ''tincture'' of ''Cocheneel'' is nothing but ſome finer ''diſſoluble'' parts of that '''Concrete''' lick'd up or ''diſſolv'd'' by the ''fluid'' water.}}
#* {{RQ:Bentley Confutation of Atheism|sermon=World 2|page=11|passage=But if Gold it ſelf be admitted, as it muſt be, for a porous '''Concrete''', the proportion of Void to Body in the texture of common Air will be ſo much the greater.}}
#* {{RQ:Johnson Dictionary|entry={{smallcaps|A"mbergris}}|page=129|column=2|passage=Some affirm it [ambergris] to be a true animal '''concrete''', formed in balls in the body of the male ſpermaceti whale, and lodged in a large oval bag over the testicles.}}

